The startup behavior is normal for many HC-SR501 PIR modules. After power is applied, the sensor needs a stabilization/calibration period and its OUT signal can change during that time. Do not treat those startup transitions as real motion.

Keep the buzzer OFF during the stabilization period, then begin reading the PIR:

```cpp
const int pirPin = 3;
const int buzzerPin = 8;

const unsigned long warmupMs = 30000;

void setup() {
  pinMode(pirPin, INPUT);
  pinMode(buzzerPin, OUTPUT);

  digitalWrite(buzzerPin, LOW);  // active-high buzzer OFF
  delay(warmupMs);
}

void loop() {
  int motion = digitalRead(pirPin);

  if (motion == HIGH) {
    digitalWrite(buzzerPin, HIGH);
  } else {
    digitalWrite(buzzerPin, LOW);
  }
}
```

The delay is not an electrical repair; t simply prevents the program from interpreting the PIR module's normal startup stabilization behavior as motion. No extra hardware is required.

Yes. The HC-SR501 OUT pin can be connected directly to an Arduino Uno digital input in this setup.

The important point is signal direction and logic thresholds:
- HC-SR501 OUT is an output from the sensor.
- Arduino D2 is used as a digital input.
- A HIGH level of about 3.3 V from the PIR is high enough to be recognized as HIGH by the ATmega328P digital input used on the Uno.
- Because the signal is going from the PIR into an input, no level shifter or series resistor is required just to translate 3.3 V logic to the Uno input.
- Do not configure D2 as OUTPUT and do not drive the PIR OUT line from the Arduino.

